FINAL ANSWER:
**The bus factor is 2.** Ownership is moderately concentrated, and the largest owner has been inactive for about 6 months.

I measured this with `git blame` on the 7 `.zig` files at HEAD, about 2,120 surviving lines. Felix Queißner appears under three name spellings, which I merged.

| Author | Surviving lines | Share | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Felix Queißner (3 aliases) | 758 | 35.8% | 2026-04-16 |
| Wen Li | 552 | 26.0% | 2026-10-27 |
| jnagpaul | 188 | 8.9% | 2023-07 |
| Vincent Rischmann | 130 | 6.1% | 2025-03 |
| mochalins | 77 | 3.6% | 2025-07 |
| Dhole | 75 | 3.5% | 2026-03 |
| Everyone else (22 people) | about 340 | about 16% | mixed |

**If Felix stopped answering questions:**
- About 36% of the current code would have no author left to ask.
- Felix is also the largest committer, with 46 of 127 non-merge commits (36%).
- No single person is above 50%, so the project doesn't depend entirely on one person.

**Why the bus factor is 2:** Felix and Wen Li together own about 62% of the code. Losing both leaves under half with a knowledgeable author. Losing only one doesn't.

**Other risks:**
- **Orphaned code:** Several contributors with sizeable shares are long gone. jnagpaul (9%) last committed in 2023, Vincent Rischmann (6%) in March 2025, and Bob Farrell (2%) in 2024. That is roughly 20% of the code whose authors are effectively unreachable, on top of Felix's share.
- **Active maintainers:** Only Wen Li, Tobias Ek and Dmitri Volkov have committed in the last 90 days. Wen Li is the only one of them with a large share of the code.
- **Dates:** One commit by Dmitri Volkov is dated 2026-11-04, which is after today's date (2026-10-24). That is probably a rebased or odd author date. It doesn't change the conclusion.

**Limits of this analysis:**
- I did not break ownership down per file or module. I also didn't cross-check how many files import each one, which the bus-factor skill's template calls for. The skill's file search also doesn't include `.zig`, so I ran the git commands directly.
- Blame credits whoever last touched a line, so mechanical changes can shift credit. The "Rename allocator to gpa" and "Move library sources to src/" commits are examples. I used `-w -M -C` to reduce this, but it isn't eliminated.
- `src/width_table.zig` is probably generated or table data, and it could skew line counts heavily. Say so if you want it excluded and I'll rerun.

I can do the per-module breakdown if you want it.
